myanmar's military government has  executed four political prisoners  including a prominent lawmaker and a  well-known democracy activist the four  men had challenged last year's military  coup now among those hanged were  a member of the elected ruling party and  the veteran activist chomin yu better  known as ko jimmy the military accused  all four men of being involved in  terrorist killings and the executions  have been widely condemned by  international observers many more  political prisoners are facing similar  charges  and for more we can cross straight to  i'm in town a journalist from myanmar  who now lives in exile welcome to dw  what more can you tell us about these  four men and the  circumstances of their executions  so far very little is known about the  actual executions um even the manner of  execution is currently unclear the  military has not confirmed how it is  they were killed but most are presuming  that it was a hanging because that is  how executions have been carried out in  the past  we do know that the families were  allowed to speak with  the four men before they were executed  on friday however they weren't told at  the time that the men were going to be  executed the military also played koi  over the weekend about whether or not  they were about to carry out the  sentences although because the families  were allowed to meet with them for the  first time since their sentencing many  had speculated that this would be  carried out today  what signal is the military junta  sending would you say by carrying out  these executions  the military has been facing quite a lot  of resistance from people within the  country including many of its own  officials as well as  soldiers police and and those who work  for the military being the target of  assassination campaigns as well as  active conflict in many areas of the  country  given that there are over a hundred  people who are now on death row uh  who have been sentenced since the  military came out uh many believe that  this is a tactic to try to install uh  instill more fear into the population uh  but it's quite unlikely that this is  going to be successful although that  although there's quite a lot of anger in  the country  um over uh 2100 people have already been  killed by the military and so many are  unlikely to be held by these recent  actions well based on what you just said  uh what is the likely effect of these  executions on the  democracy movement in myanmar  we're likely to see quite a lot of  retaliation  after the sentencing of these men to  death to the death penalty as well as  the sentencing of other prominent  activists as well as politicians there  has been retaliatory attacks on prisons  on military officials and i think we're  likely to see another wave of attacks as  retaliation for  uh these actions and i think we're  likely to see the continual erosion of  rule of law  by people who  you know may have been um  in the past more willing to accept  military rule but are now um more and  more convinced every day that uh there  will be no justice and there will be no  rule of law under this regime  you've been vehement in your criticism  of the coup of the junta you now live in  exile  um do you hope to return to myanmar one  day  i do hope to return to myanmar one day  and i do think that um given the  strength of the resistance to the  military so far um you know this is a an  institution with over a billion dollar  budget um fighting against what a  population of largely farmers  that has been largely enabled to  solidify its role even over a year after  uh their coup um i do have hope that i  will be able to return in the future  however i think that is uh  not going to be something that's going  to happen in the next uh in the short  term at least  i'm in turn to journalist from myanmar  thank you so much for your time  thank you for having me  you
 

